<h1>CHAPTER 2</h1>
<h1>EXISTING SYSTEM AND PROPOSED SYSTEM</h1>
<h3>2.1 Existing System</h3>
<p>In the conventional healthcare paradigm, patients seeking specialized medical consultations follow one of two traditional pathways: physical hospital visits or unspecialized commercial video calls. </p>
<p>In physical clinics, patients must travel substantial distances to urban centers, queue for manual paper token allocation, and attempt to explain complex, nuanced physical discomforts to physicians who often do not share their mother tongue. In bilingual consultations, family members or untrained hospital ward assistants attempt informal translation, introducing significant errors regarding dosage quantities, allergy histories, and drug contraindications.</p>
<p>When remote tele-consultations are attempted using existing commercial video software (such as Zoom, WhatsApp Video, or Google Meet), the operational friction remains high:</p>
<ul><li>Appointments are arranged via uncoordinated chat messages or phone calls without slot conflict prevention.</li></ul>
<ul><li>The video tool has no medical context: it cannot look up the patient’s past history, cannot verify the doctor’s medical registration, and cannot generate a legally binding digital prescription.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Commercial tools lack real-time regional translation models capable of handling South Asian languages (such as Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam, or Tanglish dialectical phrases).</li></ul>
<ul><li>If a critical communication impasse occurs, the doctor has no mechanism to pull a certified medical interpreter into the ongoing session without ending the meeting, locating an interpreter independently, and sharing a new meeting link.</li></ul>
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h3>2.2 Limitations of the Existing System</h3>
<ol><li><strong>Severe Language Barrier:</strong> Patients speaking regional tongues cannot express symptom onset, pain intensity, or chronic histories to non-native speaking doctors, resulting in misdiagnoses.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>Absence of Real-Time Clinical Speech Translation:</strong> Standard video tools do not provide continuous speech-to-speech translation tuned for clinical vocabularies and regional idioms.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>No Dynamic Interpreter Escalation:</strong> Translators cannot be summoned on-demand into an active consultation room; doctors must awkwardly terminate the call.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>Frequent Double-Booking Conflicts:</strong> Manual diaries and static forms fail to lock time slots atomically, resulting in overlapping patient bookings for the same practitioner.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>Lack of Structured Digital Prescriptions:</strong> Doctors scribble prescriptions on paper pads or send informal text messages, which are easily lost, illegible to pharmacists, and lack auditability.</li></ol>
<ol><li><strong>Zero Role Separation &amp; Administrative Auditing:</strong> Consumer chat apps provide no verification of doctor credentials, no patient data segregation, and no administrative moderation.</li></ol>
<div class="page-break"></div>
<h3>2.3 Proposed Solution: The MEDIBRIDGE Ecosystem</h3>
<p>MEDIBRIDGE replaces fragmented, barrier-laden tools with an all-in-one clinical communication ecosystem. By embedding real-time WebRTC communications, intelligent multilingual translation pipelines, on-demand interpreter dispatch, and verified prescription workflows into a unified architecture, MEDIBRIDGE guarantees accessible, safe, and culturally respectful healthcare.</p>
<h3>2.4 Advantages of the Proposed System</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Elimination of Language Barriers:</strong> Patients speak naturally in Tamil, Hindi, Telugu, Malayalam, Kannada, or English, while doctors receive real-time translated subtitles and voice readouts.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>Culturally Respectful Communication:</strong> The custom honorific polisher replaces rude machine translation singulars with respectful clinical address (e.g., "நீங்கள் நலமா?"), building patient trust.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>Zero-Latency In-Call Human Interpretation:</strong> When automatic translation is insufficient, certified medical interpreters join the live WebRTC call in a multi-peer mesh with one click.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>Automated Slot Reservation:</strong> High-speed atomic queries prevent overlapping reservations and enforce availability rules.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>Structured, Verifiable Digital Health Records:</strong> Every consultation yields an auditable digital trail with clinical notes, diagnosis, medication frequencies, and follow-up schedules.</li></ul>
<ul><li><strong>Role-Based Isolation:</strong> Strict JWT validation guarantees that patients only see their own prescriptions, doctors manage their own patient queues, and admins oversee safety.</li></ul>
